    "q-98189": {
      "dom_id": "step-q98189",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,-4,\\,6⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨2,\\,-4,\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,4,\\,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,4,\\,-6⟩-{⟨3t,\\,-t,\\,-2t⟩|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-3⟩-⟨6,\\,-2,\\,-4⟩\\text{ } \\\\  & =⟨-7,\\,4,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
