    "q-129268": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129268",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)+⟨3,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,6,\\,0⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨-3,\\,6,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-6,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)+⟨3,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{0}⟨3,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-6,\\,0⟩+⟨3t,\\,t,\\,-2t⟩{|}_{4}^{0} \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-6,\\,0⟩+⟨-12,\\,-4,\\,8⟩\\text{ } \\\\  & =⟨-9,\\,-10,\\,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
