    "q-129273": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129273",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨6,\\,-2,\\,4⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{4}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,2⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the adjacent intervals rule, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{4}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨3,\\,-1,\\,2⟩-⟨6,\\,-2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-9,\\,3,\\,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
