    "q-129288": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129288",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Given that \n${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{e}{2}\\,i+3e\\,j-\\,k,$ \n$c=4\\,i-j+u\\,k,$\nand \n${\\int }_{1}^{3}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ what is the value of $u?$",
      "worked_solution": "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(4\\,i-j+u\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{e}{2}\\,i+3e\\,j-k) \\\\  & =2e-3e-u \\\\  & =-e-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\int }_{1}^{3}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ we must have $-e-u=0,$ which means that $u=-e.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
