    "q-32521": {
      "dom_id": "step-q32521",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=16\\,i+8\\,j+2\\,k$ \nand \n$c=-i+j.$",
      "worked_solution": "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-i+j)\\times (16\\,i+8\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 8 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 16 & 8\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(-2-0)j+(-8-16)k \\\\  & =2i+2j-24k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
